  <h2>Abstract</h2>
  <p>As general AI agents displace large segments of human labor, traditional income-generation models will erode. This thesis proposes a new paradigm where personal AI agents autonomously transact and monetize an individual's self-sovereign data in micro-payment marketplaces, generating a form of Universal Basic Income (UBI) without government redistribution. We call this <strong>Agentic Universal Basic Income (AUBI)</strong>.</p>

  <h2>Displacement of human labor by the agentic economy</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Widespread automation.</strong> General-purpose AI agents will perform not only physical tasks but also cognitive, creative, and service work, reducing demand for human labor across white collar and blue collar jobs.</li>
    <li><strong>Shrinking wage economy.</strong> As automation lowers marginal costs, corporations will optimize for efficiency, not employment, leading to structural unemployment.</li>
    <li><strong>Rise of digital rentierism.</strong> Those who control scarce digital assets (IP, data, compute, algorithms) will accumulate wealth, while laborers are increasingly excluded from income flows.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>From tax redistribution to agentic value creation</h2>
  <p><strong>Traditional UBI:</strong> funded by state redistribution (e.g., carbon tax, robot tax, wealth tax); politically difficult to scale or sustain; treats individuals as passive recipients.</p>
  <p><strong>Agentic UBI (AUBI):</strong> generated by individual-owned AI agents negotiating data transactions in real time; built on self-sovereign data, zero-knowledge proof sharing, and programmable consent; powered by decentralized micro-payment protocols and tokenized marketplaces; reduces dependence on state transfers.</p>

  <h2>Agentic Universal Basic Income infrastructure</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>SSI wallet.</strong> Stores identity credentials and permissions for data sharing.</li>
    <li><strong>Personal AI agent.</strong> Negotiates on behalf of the user to monetize data, attention, and tasks.</li>
    <li><strong>Data vault.</strong> Secure store for structured personal data (health, browsing, IoT, etc.).</li>
    <li><strong>ZKP layer.</strong> Ensures data verification without exposure.</li>
    <li><strong>Tokenized payments.</strong> Executes micro-payments in crypto or fiat for data usage or insights.</li>
    <li><strong>Consent engine.</strong> Lets users define policies (e.g., sell anonymized fitness data only).</li>
    <li><strong>Agent reputation layer.</strong> Builds trust between autonomous agents across networks.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Monetizable data streams</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Health and fitness metrics.</strong> Personal health data from wearables and medical records that can be anonymized and monetized for research and development.</li>
    <li><strong>Browsing behavior.</strong> Online activity patterns that provide valuable insights for marketers and product developers.</li>
    <li><strong>Mobility patterns.</strong> Smart car telemetry and location data that can inform urban planning and transportation services.</li>
    <li><strong>Purchase and attention data.</strong> Consumer preferences and engagement metrics valuable to retailers and advertisers.</li>
    <li><strong>Professional credentials.</strong> Verified skills and qualifications that can be shared with potential employers or service platforms.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Economic and policy implications</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>From welfare state to self-monetizing citizen.</strong> AUBI shifts economic agency back to the individual, allowing citizens to generate income from previously unmonetized digital behavior. Eliminates reliance on the welfare bureaucracy or taxation for income redistribution.</li>
    <li><strong>A new tax base: autonomous agent markets.</strong> Governments could tax the marketplaces (not individuals), ensuring economic activity remains trackable and ethical while preserving privacy.</li>
    <li><strong>Incentivizing open data ecosystems.</strong> Data cooperatives and DAOs can aggregate user data and negotiate better returns for contributors, creating a fairer alternative to Big Tech platforms.</li>
    <li><strong>Post-labor legitimacy of the state.</strong> States could transition from employment-based legitimacy to infrastructure-providing legitimacy (e.g., secure identity, agent licensing, auditability).</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Thesis contribution</h2>
  <p>This model positions human data as the "new labor," and personal AI agents as its "workers".</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A sustainable, privacy-respecting income layer.</strong> Creating economic value while maintaining individual control over personal data through consent mechanisms and zero-knowledge proofs.</li>
    <li><strong>A mechanism for inclusion in an AI-driven economy.</strong> Ensuring all individuals can participate in and benefit from automation and AI advancement, regardless of traditional employment status.</li>
    <li><strong>A decentralized alternative to tax-funded UBI models.</strong> Reducing dependence on centralized government redistribution by enabling direct value capture from personal data assets.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Summary</h2>
  <p>In a world where AI displaces human labor, self-sovereign data plus agentic automation offers a powerful replacement for traditional income: <strong>Agentic Universal Basic Income (AUBI)</strong>. Unlike welfare-based UBI, AUBI is funded by the digital productivity of a person's AI agents negotiating on their behalf.</p>
  <p>This framework transforms identity, behavior, and consent into programmable capital, ushering in a decentralized and user-controlled economic future.</p>
</Thesis>
